Manage attached resources such as screenshots, docs, logs, and other files.

Use "task-board resource add" to create a new attached resource, "task-board
resource adopt" to register an orphaned local payload, "task-board resource
update" to replace an existing one, "task-board resource delete" to remove it,
and "task-board resource get" to materialize a resource back to the local
filesystem for inspection.

Usage:
  task-board resource [command]

Available Commands:
  add         Attach a new file resource to an element
  adopt       Register an existing unreferenced local resource payload
  delete      Delete a resource from an element (requires --confirm)
  get         Download a resource to the local filesystem
  update      Replace an existing file resource on an element

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for resource

Global Flags:
      --board-dir string         Path to the board directory (overrides TASK_BOARD_DIR and config local.board_dir) (default ".task-board")
      --context-profile string   Agent context profile override: full or lite
      --insecure                 Skip TLS certificate verification (for self-signed certs)
      --json                     Output in JSON format
      --no-update-check          Disable automatic update check
      --remote string            Remote board server URL (overrides TASK_BOARD_REMOTE)
      --remote-board string      Remote board ID (overrides TASK_BOARD_ID)

Use "task-board resource [command] --help" for more information about a command.
Fix BUG-260917-3lddu0 (sessrepo-accepts-stale-lease-append-while-tail-matches), the **FINAL leaf** of STORY-260917-kf9g1h (landed-gate-hardening-from-independent-review). The Story's other leaves are done: BUG-260918-354b03 landed earlier, and BUG-260917-2fwf8e (fencing arm ordering) is a signed checkpoint commit `964fa47` on the Story branch. Your Change Request will be **story_final** and will therefore carry the WHOLE Story — that checkpoint plus your leaf.

THE DEFECT. RUN-260917-3934e0 (claude-opus-5 max) found it in probes 9 and 15 while reviewing TASK-260830-1geqhj CR1; both probes are inside `TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-evidence-rev1.tar.gz`. Read them and reproduce both on trunk `799c338` before you write anything. `internal/sessrepo`'s `checkAppend` (chain.go:166) admits an append under a **superseded** lease as long as the chain tail still sits on that lease — `ErrStaleLease` fires only after the tail moves (chain.go:169 compares the event's epoch against the TAIL's epoch, not against the winning lease). Probe 15 is the blast radius and it is why this is not a bookkeeping bug: a `profile.changed` event appended under losing lease A **after successor A2 already won** is accepted, and then drives the effective profile — to `yolo` with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`. Pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md` §2.4 (line 646) forbids exactly that: losing-lease or ambiguous events MUST NOT change the effective profile. A losing writer can therefore turn off approvals and the sandbox on a session it no longer owns.

WHAT TO DELIVER. Refuse the append under a superseded lease even when the tail still sits on it, and make the §2.4 property hold independently of the append gate — a losing-lease `profile.changed` must never be the effective profile source even if some other path admits the event. Two properties, two sets of tests: do not let one of them stand in for the other, because a reviewer will plant against each separately. Scope: `internal/sessrepo` append admission; **the writers that compose it must keep working unchanged**. If the fix appears to need a change outside that boundary, STOP and say so in the results rather than widening.

**BUILD THE CENSUS FROM THE START — THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS WHY EARLIER LEAVES ON THIS EPIC TOOK SIX ROUNDS.** The recurring defect on this codebase is not wrong code, it is a rule witnessed at ONE of the several production entries that reach it, so a narrowing at any other entry walks through a green suite. Ship a **gate × entry census** as its own table in the conformance matrix, mirrored in TRACEABILITY: for every gate you touch, list every production entry that can reach it, and give each cell exactly one of — a named committed test driving the gate THROUGH that entry plus an admitting narrowing row it kills; `unreachable` with the reason; or a stated BOUND naming what is unmeasured and who owns it. "Pinned at the helper" is not a cell: a helper pin plus an entry that reaches the same gate is two cells. And for any gate implemented on two or more sides, compare the row sets side by side and report a symmetry line — a gate whose sides have different row counts is a finding waiting to happen. Report the measured cell ratio.

THE LANDED-CODE HAZARD, AND THE INSTRUMENT THAT MISSES IT. You are changing admission in landed code with landed tests. An admission change moves inputs across arms silently: a previously-admitted input can start being refused by a DIFFERENT arm, or vice versa, while the suite stays green because some other test already covered that code. On this Story's other leaf a **name-keyed per-test diff scoped to one package reported "only additions" while eight rows in another package changed meaning under unchanged names** — that is how a regression reached review. So compare **outcomes**, not test names, and over the complete importer set of `internal/sessrepo`, not just `internal/sessrepo` itself (derive the importer list mechanically and put it in the evidence). Name every moved class in the results with its before and after and say whether it is intended. If an existing test encodes the old admission as an expectation, changing it is part of the fix and must be argued explicitly with a spec citation — rewriting a landed regression test to accept new behaviour without one is the single fastest way to get this revision rejected.

STORY-FINAL RESPONSIBILITIES (this leaf, not the other one). Edit `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` — the ADOPTED registry, v0.6.0 is no longer adopted and editing it would be invisible — adding this Story's bindings; every binding must name a clause an executed test actually drives. **Re-derive `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`** from the registry as it stands in your candidate; a digest copied from another Story or left stale is a P1. Run `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` and the section-scoped runs on the exact candidate tree and quote the ratios verbatim. The README "Measured coverage of this repository" subsection must equal what tracecheck prints on your tree — the landed `readme_coverage_pin_test.go` pins it, so confirm the test is actually exercised. LOGBOOK entry newest-first and factually true. If trunk moves before your handoff another Story may have touched the same registry: run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate BUG-260917-3lddu0`, then **MERGE the registry, never replace it** — trunk's rows must all still be present alongside yours — and re-derive the digest again after the merge.

CARRY-FORWARD FROM THE SIBLING LEAF'S VERDICT. Four P3 notes were recorded for you; disposition each in the results. (1) `internal/axpane/decide.go` doc comments (`authorize` line 585, `fencingClass` line 1063) still say "lapsed grants refuse", which is now true only for a local owner — fold the qualifier in if you touch `axpane`, and if you do not, say so. (2) `observeRemoteWinner`'s whole remote-winner dispatch is structurally redundant: deleting it keeps the complete importer set green twice, because the outer `StaleRelativeToWinner` fallback produces the same outcome. **Record this only — do not do the simplification in this leaf.** (3) A remote interactive owner with NO grant at all refuses `invalid_arguments` before direction on both trees; whether §4.2 step 4 must also be reachable with `HasGrant=false` is an unresolved product question for the leaf that mints grants. Record it as unresolved; do not decide it. (4) Evidence hygiene the sibling got wrong: record the candidate TREE OID, not the base commit, and state the test mask beside any row count you quote.

DO NOT FORK A LANDED OWNER. `internal/sessrepo` composes with `internal/sessstate`, `internal/fencing` and the lease model. "Under the winning lease" means another host's lease when the winner is remote. Delegate to the landed owners; do not re-model leases, epochs or digests locally.

EVIDENCE CONTRACT (repository DoD). Every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; a negative for every refusal, asserting the literal spec token rather than a production constant; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (`PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, no `__pycache__` anywhere); crash and idempotency evidence for every durable write, or an explicit purity bound; the conformance matrix with the census table; a LOGBOOK entry newest-first.

WORKSPACE. The managed Story worktree `.temp/STORY-260917-kf9g1h/worktree` ALREADY EXISTS on branch `task-board/story/STORY-260917-kf9g1h` and its tip is the sibling's signed checkpoint `964fa47` — do not re-provision it, do not rebase it, do not commit on it, do not amend or revert the checkpoint, never edit `.task-board` directly and never self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint: exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Read the sibling's landed delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260917-kf9g1h/worktree show --stat HEAD`) and its outcome resources first so you extend it rather than restate it. Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths.

PROCESS. Read the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill first. All CI is local; never trigger hosted CI. Run your own package tests, the importer set, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once, reading the commands from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` in the candidate config (30 of them). Command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m`; another Story is producing concurrently on this host, so expect it to be slow and do NOT shorten it — if a gate times out, report the measured elapsed time and host load rather than arguing it away. Report the coverage ratio you MEASURED. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely. Attach BUG-260917-3lddu0_results.md, BUG-260917-3lddu0_conformance-matrix.md and BUG-260917-3lddu0_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff BUG-260917-3lddu0 --role developer` and exit promptly. **Resolve every artifact path ABSOLUTELY and verify what you attached by reading it back off the board**: the control root and the Story worktree both have a `.temp/BUG-260917-3lddu0/`, and the sibling leaf lost a round by attaching the worktree's stale copies. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: gpt-5.6-luna max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.
REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.
(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.
(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.
(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.
(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.
(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.
(f) Prose that outruns the tests. "Composes X", "never admits what decoding refuses", "nothing re-implemented", "no cached sentinel can authorize" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.
Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.

